## 4. Remove forced re-entry of an already supplied address

This maps directly to **3.3.7 Redundant Entry, Level A**. Information entered earlier in the **same process** that must be supplied again must be either automatically populated or available for the user to select. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="20"}

[W3C — Understanding 3.3.7 Redundant Entry](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/redundant-entry?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For checkout, W3C specifically gives the pattern of letting the customer select that the billing and delivery addresses are the same. It also documents a checkbox that populates one address from the earlier one as a sufficient technique. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="22"}

[W3C — G221: Provide data from a previous step in a process](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Techniques/general/G221?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Three exceptions matter: re-entry can be required where it is **essential**, required for **security**, or where the previously supplied information is **no longer valid**. Ordinary repetition of a shipping address does not obviously fit any of them. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="24"}

One nuance prevents overcalling the failure. Shipping address and billing address are legitimately two different pieces of information because they can differ. The accessibility problem arises when the user needs the **same values again** and is forced to reproduce them manually. A “Billing address same as shipping” control followed by editable populated values handles both possibilities.

Also, **browser autocomplete alone does not satisfy 3.3.7**. W3C says the website/content must make the previously entered information available; that is distinct from browser autofill. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="25"}

**Acceptance test:** enter address A once, advance through the same checkout process, and choose the same-address path. No address component should need to be recalled and manually retyped. Confirm that users can still alter the second address when it genuinely differs.

### A practical two-week sequence

I would spend the first day verifying the four pass/fail conditions above rather than opening four tickets based solely on their descriptions. Then implement the quantity-control and sticky-footer changes first because they can make checkout operation impossible or effectively blind for particular input users. Next make the target-size correction centrally in the shared control CSS if possible; one component change may repair many targets. Then add the address reuse mechanism.

Use the second week for cross-interaction regression rather than adding more speculative WCAG work: full keyboard traversal, mouse/touch without dragging, target-size measurements across breakpoints, the same/different billing-address paths, validation errors, and responsive/zoom states.

A useful release gate would be:

1. **Quantity:** every value selectable with keyboard and with click/tap only; no dragging required.
2. **Focus:** no author-created sticky content completely hides a focused component; preferably no part of it or its indicator is covered.
3. **Targets:** every applicable pointer target is ≥24×24 CSS px or has a documented, actually verified WCAG exception.
4. **Address:** information already supplied during checkout can be selected or populated rather than retyped, unless one of 3.3.7's explicit exceptions applies.

The main unresolved uncertainty is implementation detail, not WCAG interpretation: whether the footer covers components completely, whether “18px” describes artwork or hit boxes, whether the slider already has hidden alternative operations, and whether the two address requests really require identical information. Those four checks can change the formal failure count, but they do **not** materially change the remediation plan above.

