# B. Expanded findings

### 1. Sticky footer: 2.4.11 is narrower than “never cover focus”

The AA requirement is that a component receiving keyboard focus is **not entirely hidden** by author-created content. A footer covering 20% of the control may be poor UX yet still satisfy 2.4.11. The stricter rule that **no part** may be obscured is **2.4.12 Focus Not Obscured (Enhanced), Level AAA**. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="14"}

There are two important qualifications. User-movable content is assessed in its initial position. Content explicitly opened by the user can obscure a focused component if the user can expose it without advancing focus. Those qualifications generally do **not** rescue a normal author-positioned sticky checkout footer; W3C specifically uses sticky footers as the failure example. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="15"}

Also check **2.4.7 Focus Visible (AA)**. If the component itself technically remains partially visible but the only visible focus indicator is hidden, you may pass 2.4.11 yet still have a focus-visibility problem. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="16"}

### 2. Slider: there are two independent accessibility obligations

**2.5.7** does not say “make dragging keyboard accessible.” It says functionality that uses dragging must also be achievable by a **single pointer without dragging** unless an exception applies. W3C explicitly warns that a keyboard implementation by itself does not satisfy this requirement. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="17"}

Separately, **2.1.1 Keyboard** requires the underlying functionality to be keyboard operable unless the function inherently depends on the path of movement. Selecting a quantity does not inherently require a path, so a truly drag-only quantity mechanism would normally need a keyboard alternative too. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="18"}

Acceptable non-drag designs include clicking the slider track, `−`/`+` buttons, or entering the value into a text/number field. W3C specifically identifies a text input next to a slider as a valid single-pointer alternative. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="19"}

If this is a **custom** slider, inspect one additional Level A requirement: **4.1.2 Name, Role, Value**. Its accessible role, minimum/maximum/current values, label, and changes must be exposed to assistive technology. Native standard controls substantially reduce that implementation burden. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="20"}

### 3. 18px icons: measure the target, not the artwork

The AA threshold in WCAG 2.2 is **24×24 CSS pixels**, not 44×44. A target smaller than that can alternatively pass through spacing: W3C's test conceptually centers a 24px-diameter circle on every undersized target and requires it not to intersect another target or another undersized target's circle. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="21"}

For two neighboring 18×18 square hit targets in a row, that implies roughly **6 CSS px between their active boxes** for their circle centers to be 24px apart. Zero spacing would not meet the spacing exception. But again, if only the *glyph* is 18px and CSS padding makes the clickable button 24×24 or larger, it passes the size branch directly.

Five exceptions exist: sufficient **spacing**, an **equivalent** conforming control, an **inline** text target, a target whose size is controlled by the **user agent** and unmodified by the author, or an **essential** presentation. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="22"}

For checkout icon buttons, simply enlarging the hit area is usually less fragile than trying to prove a spacing exception.

### 4. Retyping the address: browser autocomplete alone is not enough

**3.3.7 Redundant Entry** requires information already entered or supplied in the same process to be auto-populated or available for the user to select. Its explicit exceptions are re-entry that is essential, needed for security, or necessary because the old information is no longer valid. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="23"}

W3C specifically states that ordinary browser autocomplete does **not** by itself satisfy 3.3.7; the website must retain/provide the previously entered information or stop asking for it again. It also explicitly identifies a “billing address same as shipping address” checkbox as an acceptable approach. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="24"}

The same checkout process can span domains. W3C says a third-party payment provider included in a checkout can still be part of the process for this criterion. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="25"}

At the same time, address fields should also be checked against **1.3.5 Identify Input Purpose (AA)**. For HTML, correct `autocomplete` purpose tokens such as shipping/billing address components are a standard technique. That requirement is different from redundant entry: autocomplete metadata identifies what the field means; it does not substitute for retaining data already supplied earlier in the current process. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="26"}

