# C. Questions you would probably have asked next

### “Do we need every target to be 24×24?”

No. **24×24 is one way to pass 2.5.8**, not the only one. The spacing and four other exceptions remain available. That said, sizing checkout buttons to at least 24×24 is much easier to test and maintain than continuously reasoning about clearance geometry. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="27"}

### “Isn't the WCAG target-size rule 44×44?”

Not at WCAG 2.2 AA. **2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum), AA is 24×24 CSS px or an exception.** The older/larger 44×44 threshold is associated with the enhanced target-size requirement. Current W3C's WCAG 2.2 C42 technique uses 24px. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="28"}

### “Do arrow keys on the slider solve dragging?”

Only half of the problem. They can solve the keyboard requirement, but **2.5.7 still needs a single-pointer method that does not involve dragging**. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="29"}

### “Can the sticky footer cover just the focus ring?”

2.4.11 measures whether the **component** is completely hidden, rather than simply whether an external focus ring is hidden. But a hidden focus indicator may separately implicate **2.4.7 Focus Visible**. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="30"}

### “Must billing address always be copied from shipping?”

No. Users must remain able to specify a genuinely different billing address. The requirement is to avoid making them *re-enter information that is already the same*. A “same as shipping” option followed by editable billing fields satisfies that distinction. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="31"}

