# D. Important things you might not have known to ask about

**Focus Appearance is not an AA requirement in the final WCAG 2.2 Recommendation.** 2.4.13 Focus Appearance is AAA. So for this audit, do not accidentally use draft-era expectations as an AA blocker. Focus Visible remains AA, while Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) is the new AA criterion relevant here. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="32"}

Also, **W3C Understanding documents and Techniques are informative**, not themselves normative requirements. They explain how the normative success criterion should be interpreted and give known sufficient/failure techniques. That matters when an implementation differs from the examples: conformance is ultimately judged against the success criterion, not whether you copied W3C's specific technique. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="33"}

For the custom slider, accessible semantics deserve explicit testing. A widget can satisfy “can be dragged/clicked/keyboard-operated” yet still be unusable to a screen reader if its role and current quantity are not programmatically exposed. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="34"}

Finally, test the **error path**, not only the happy path. For example, if submission reports an invalid card or address and your code clears previously entered valid fields, you can reintroduce redundant-entry problems. W3C's 3.3.7 examples specifically retain entered information after checkout validation errors. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="35"}

