Assuming the house is in Germany: **a cold climate by itself is not a reason to reject a heat pump.** I would lean toward buying one **if your house can stay comfortable with a heating-system flow temperature around 55°C or below on the coldest days**. That is the most useful go/no-go test. German consumer and government guidance uses roughly this threshold because heat-pump efficiency deteriorates as required water temperature rises. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

Recent German field evidence is encouraging. Fraunhofer ISE's 2024 measurements found 49 air-to-water systems averaging a seasonal performance factor/JAZ of **3.4**, while 13 ground-source systems achieved **3.6–5.4**. Importantly, Fraunhofer did not find a relationship between building age and efficiency in that sample; required heating temperatures were much more important. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"} Modern heat pumps can also supply heat at outdoor temperatures down to roughly −20°C, although air-source efficiency declines as outdoor temperatures fall. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}

My decision rule would be:

- **Good candidate:** ≤55°C flow temperature at design-cold conditions; ideally 35–50°C. Existing oversized radiators or underfloor heating are favorable. Proceed to proper heat-loss calculation and quotations.
- **Borderline but often worth fixing:** a few rooms need higher temperatures. Larger radiators, fan-assisted radiators, hydraulic balancing, or targeted insulation can often bring the whole system into an efficient range; a full-house renovation is not automatically necessary. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}
- **Poor candidate for a straightforward air-source replacement:** the house genuinely needs 65–75°C for long periods and you cannot reduce the heat loss or enlarge the emitters. High-temperature heat pumps can technically provide much hotter water, but doing so sacrifices efficiency. Ground-source or a staged/hybrid approach deserves comparison in that case. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

For the financial side, ignore headline COP figures and compare **delivered heat cost** using the quoted seasonal performance:

`heat-pump heat cost = electricity price ÷ expected JAZ`

versus roughly

`gas heat cost = gas price ÷ boiler seasonal efficiency`.

A JAZ of 3.4 means roughly 3.4 kWh of heat for each kWh of electricity over the year. Your actual electricity/gas tariffs therefore matter nearly as much as the equipment price. Ask every installer to state the assumed **design heat load, design flow temperature, expected JAZ/SCOP and annual electricity use**; a quote giving only the heat pump's nominal kW rating is not enough.

If you are in Germany, incentives materially alter the calculation. As of September 2026, KfW's program 458 advertises **30–80% support on eligible costs**, subject to the applicant and bonus conditions; for a one-unit house, KfW currently uses up to **€28,000 of eligible expenditure**. The scheme was changed on 21 July 2026, so use the current KfW conditions rather than an older installer brochure. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="5"}

A particularly useful thing you can do **before spending anything** is test your existing heating system during genuinely cold weather: open the radiators, disable night setback, lower the heating curve progressively, and see whether the house remains comfortable at about **55°C maximum flow temperature**. Verbraucherzentrale describes essentially this test as a practical indicator of heat-pump suitability. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="6"} The Umweltbundesamt also now provides a room-by-room heat-pump building check that evaluates heat load and radiator capacity. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="7"}

So my answer is **probably yes if you pass that temperature/heat-load test; don't buy purely on an installer's assurance that a “high-temperature model works in any house.”** In a cold-climate house, correct sizing and low distribution temperatures matter more than the label “cold climate.”

If you give me your **annual gas/oil consumption, heated floor area, current cold-day flow temperature, radiator vs. floor heating, electricity price, and one or two installation quotes**, I can turn this into a fairly concrete heat-pump versus current-heating cost comparison.

