# C. Questions you would probably ask next

**“Should we simply inspect whichever town is nearer the epicenter?”**  
No. Distance from the rupture is important, but “distance from the epicenter” is an imperfect shortcut. Finite fault geometry, rupture direction, earthquake depth and local soil conditions can all change shaking. USGS explicitly describes intensity as depending on distance from the **fault rupture area**, along with these additional factors. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="17"}

**“Should the town with the higher ShakeMap MMI always go first?”**  
Not necessarily. Higher shaking raises concern, but consequence also depends on exposure and vulnerability. A smaller population of earthquake-resistant buildings experiencing stronger shaking may present a different life-safety problem from a dense population of vulnerable buildings experiencing somewhat less shaking. This is exactly why PAGER combines hazard, exposure and vulnerability. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="18"}

**“Can PAGER make the town-level decision for us?”**  
Use it as screening evidence, not as an automatic dispatch algorithm. PAGER provides rapid estimates of populations exposed, fatalities and economic losses, but USGS explicitly warns about uncertainties in population, earthquake parameters and loss modeling. Its estimates can also omit important secondary losses. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="19"}

**“What if reliable reports say buildings have collapsed before ShakeMap is well constrained?”**  
Those observations materially change the decision. Models are especially useful in the information vacuum immediately following an earthquake; confirmed life-safety consequences are more direct evidence of where inspection or rescue capacity may be needed.

**“What if the models disagree?”**  
Look for why. ShakeMap represents shaking; PAGER adds population and vulnerability; Ground Failure addresses landslide/liquefaction potential; direct reports observe actual consequences. Disagreement may therefore be informative rather than erroneous.

