# D. Important things you might not have known to ask

One significant blind spot is **observation density**. A town with several nearby accelerometers or many credible DYFI reports may have a much better constrained ShakeMap value than an equally affected remote town with almost no observations. “No reported damage” can mean “little damage,” but it can also mean **communications failure or missing observers**. The ShakeMap uncertainty/grid and input-station information help distinguish those cases. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="20"}

A second is **within-town variation**. ShakeMap contours are regional estimates, and USGS explicitly cautions that shaking can vary substantially over short distances. A single town-wide MMI label therefore may conceal strongly different conditions across neighborhoods or sites. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="21"}

A third is **building-frequency response**. For facility inspection, a general intensity value is not necessarily the most discriminating engineering measure. ShakeCast can use ground-motion measures and structural fragility functions because different structures respond differently to different frequencies of shaking. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="22"}

A fourth is **time evolution**. Initial location, depth, rupture geometry, ShakeMap and PAGER estimates can change substantially as observations arrive. The priority decision therefore needs explicit reassessment rather than being made once from the first bulletin. USGS says PAGER estimates generally improve as sensor data and reported intensities are incorporated and recommends using the current version. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="23"}

