# E. Contradictions and alternative explanations

There is no contradiction between saying **magnitude matters** and saying **magnitude is insufficient**. Magnitude strongly influences the potential size and geographic extent of shaking and is an important ShakeMap/PAGER input. The error would be treating it as a town-specific damage measurement. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="25"}

Similarly, “strongest shaking first” and “largest expected consequences first” can produce different priorities. The former is a pure hazard rule; the latter incorporates exposure and vulnerability. USGS's impact-oriented products support the latter framework: PAGER combines those factors, while ShakeCast combines shaking and facility fragility for inspection prioritization. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="26"}

A model may also disagree with eyewitness reports without either necessarily being useless. Sparse observations can leave ShakeMap poorly constrained, while individual human reports can be subjective or unrepresentative. As observations accumulate, ShakeMap incorporates instrumental and macroseismic information and may change. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="27"}

One particularly important counterexample to an epicenter-based rule is a same-magnitude deep versus shallow earthquake. USGS compares events of roughly equal magnitude and shows that the shallower earthquake generated substantially stronger surface shaking. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="28"}

