### FIPS 204 versus FIPS 205

| Issue | FIPS 204 | FIPS 205 |
|---|---|---|
| Algorithm | ML-DSA | SLH-DSA |
| Foundation | Module-lattice | Stateless hash-based |
| NIST role | Primary PQ signature standard | Diversity/backup signature standard |
| Standard signature sizes | 2,420–4,627 bytes | 7,856–49,856 bytes |
| Important consequence | Usually easier starting point for general software signing | Different security foundation, but bandwidth/storage can be much heavier |

The size difference is not cosmetic. It can alter signed-container layouts, boot ROM parsers, fixed firmware fields, OTA transfer cost, flash allocation and verification latency. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="19"}

NIST's 2026 draft work on limited-use SLH-DSA parameter sets is particularly revealing: it explicitly targets software/firmware signing because the normal FIPS 205 parameters can have undesirable signature-size or performance characteristics. That is evidence that **application engineering remains part of PQ migration**, not evidence that FIPS 205 is defective. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="20"}

### Migration should change the verifier before relying on the new signer

A robust sequence is:

**current verification → add PQ capability → deploy it broadly → dual-sign/parallel-test where useful → establish recovery confidence → require PQ on upgraded populations → eventually remove classical acceptance.**

That sequence follows the coexistence model NIST anticipates during the transition. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="21"}

A particularly important failure mode is incorrect “hybrid” semantics. If a package carries a classical and a PQ signature but the new verifier accepts the package when **either one** verifies, a future break of the classical algorithm still gives an attacker a successful path. NIST's dual-signature discussion describes verification in which all component signatures are successfully verified. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="22"}

That does not mean every deployment must use permanent dual signatures. They add implementation complexity, update size and another transition to manage. NIST explicitly notes that hybrid mechanisms can increase cost and complexity and are generally transition techniques rather than automatically the final architecture. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="23"}

### Crypto agility is part of the deliverable

NIST's current CSWP 39 update defines crypto agility as the ability to replace/adapt cryptographic algorithms across software, firmware, hardware, protocols and infrastructure while maintaining operation. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="24"}

For your update design, that implies avoiding an immutable assumption such as “signature = ECDSA-P256 forever” or even “signature = ML-DSA-44 forever.” Algorithm IDs, key identifiers, lengths and trust policy need versionable representations; parsers need strict bounds checking; and key/algorithm retirement needs to be possible without redesigning the update format.

