The size difference is not theoretical: FIPS 204 specifies 2,420-, 3,309-, and 4,627-byte signatures for ML-DSA-44/65/87, while FIPS 205's standard SLH-DSA sets range from 7,856 to 49,856 bytes. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"} NIST originally recommended Dilithium as its primary signature choice because of its security/performance characteristics, while describing SPHINCS+ as valuable diversification against a future problem affecting lattice cryptography. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}

That makes **ML-DSA the sensible first implementation to evaluate**, not an automatic choice for every device. SLH-DSA becomes more attractive when algorithmic diversity is especially important and its substantially larger signatures and computation fit your envelope. NIST is also investigating additional signature algorithms, so designing your update format around an algorithm identifier rather than permanently hard-coding one primitive is important crypto-agility. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="4"}

### Conditional migration decision

A rapid production migration is warranted if you have long-lived devices whose verification code or trust anchors cannot later be changed, especially if some will remain supported for many years. NIST explicitly calls out this code-signing case. Conversely, if every verifier is reliably updateable, your fleet turns over relatively quickly, and you can stage new verification logic safely, an immediate fleet-wide cutover is much less defensible than a controlled introduction and measurement first. That second conclusion is an engineering inference from NIST's code-signing guidance, not a NIST-prescribed timetable. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="5"}

A transitional **dual-signature** arrangement can sometimes help: retain the existing classical signature while introducing a PQ signature for newly capable verifiers. NIST explicitly accommodates dual/hybrid signatures, but warns that hybrid approaches add implementation complexity, cost, and security risk and may be temporary transition mechanisms rather than permanent architecture. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="6"} An important failure mode is turning this into an indefinite "`classical OR PQ`" acceptance rule: an updated verifier that continues accepting a forgeable classical signature alone would not gain the intended future quantum resistance. The exact compatibility design therefore has to follow your update-container format and verifier behavior.

### What to establish before choosing the rollout

For a five-person team, the highest-value first work is evidence collection rather than simultaneous implementation on every product. NIST's NCCoE migration work explicitly starts with **cryptographic visibility/inventory** and **interoperability/benchmarking**. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="7"} I would collect, in this order:

